== 8.1 Development and Reproductive Health

Puberty is a period of physical and emotional development associated with hormonal changes. Changes can include growth, body hair, changes in voice or body shape, menstruation and sperm production. Timing and experiences differ; a difference from a classmate does not automatically indicate a problem. Persistent concerns, severe pain or unusual symptoms can be discussed with an appropriately qualified professional.

Menstruation is the periodic shedding of the uterine lining when pregnancy has not occurred, as part of a reproductive cycle. Cycle length and experience vary, especially during adolescence. Access to clean menstrual materials, water, privacy and safe disposal supports dignity and participation. Menstruation is not a reason to shame or exclude someone.

#rule-box("Development varies")[
  A diagram and a typical pattern are educational models, not a diagnosis. People develop at different rates and can seek qualified advice when concerned.
]

== 8.2 Relationships, Consent and Safety

Healthy relationships involve respect, communication and attention to boundaries. *Consent* means a freely given agreement to a specific activity. Silence, pressure, fear, intoxication or a previous agreement do not establish consent. A person can withdraw consent; the other person must respect that decision. Laws and safeguarding procedures vary, including rules concerning age and capacity, so seek trusted local guidance where needed.

Coercion, threats or unwanted sexual contact are not the affected person's fault. A person experiencing harm can seek help from a trusted adult, health professional or appropriate support service. If there is immediate danger, seek local emergency assistance. Respect privacy, but do not promise secrecy that prevents necessary safeguarding when someone is at risk.

