== 8.3 Pregnancy Prevention and STI Protection

Family planning supports informed decisions about whether and when to have children. Contraceptive methods differ in how they work, how they are used, effectiveness, suitability and access. Some are long-acting, some are used regularly, and condoms are barrier methods. A qualified professional can explain options in relation to an individual's health, preferences and circumstances. No single method is the best choice for everyone.

Correct and consistent use of external or internal condoms reduces the risk of HIV and many other sexually transmitted infections (STIs) and can help prevent pregnancy. Most other contraceptive methods prevent pregnancy but do not protect against STIs. Condoms do not eliminate every risk, including infections spread through skin not covered by the condom. Abstaining from sexual activity avoids exposure through that activity. Testing, vaccination where appropriate, and timely care are additional parts of prevention.

#concept-box("Two different questions")[
  Ask separately: does the method help prevent pregnancy, and does it reduce STI transmission? A method can address one without addressing the other.
]

== 8.4 Reliable Information and Support

STIs can occur without obvious symptoms. A person's appearance does not establish infection status; testing and appropriate care are important when indicated. Do not share medication or assume one treatment fits every infection. A person seeking help deserves respectful, non-judgemental care and privacy subject to applicable safeguarding requirements.

Use current public-health information and appropriately qualified advice rather than relying on rumours or a single online claim. In Nigeria and elsewhere, the availability of services and laws can vary. Check local, current guidance. This study material is educational and does not replace individual clinical or legal advice.

== 8.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "Puberty", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [Which statement about puberty is most accurate? \
A. Development and timing vary between individuals \
B. Everyone develops on exactly the same date \
C. A difference from a classmate always proves illness \
D. Puberty involves no physical changes],
  [Puberty varies in timing and experience. A concern can be discussed with a qualified professional. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "Consent", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A person agrees to an activity and then says they want to stop. What should happen? \
A. The previous agreement must override the new response \
B. Stop and respect the withdrawal of consent \
C. Continue because silence is consent \
D. Apply pressure until the person agrees again],
  [Consent can be withdrawn and the decision must be respected. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Protection", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [Which statement distinguishes contraception and STI prevention? \
A. Every contraceptive method protects equally against STIs \
B. Pregnancy prevention and STI prevention are identical \
C. Condoms reduce risk of many STIs as well as helping prevent pregnancy when used correctly and consistently \
D. Appearance proves STI status],
  [Condoms can address both pregnancy and many STI risks; most other contraceptive methods do not protect against STIs. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Seeking support", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A learner reports unwanted sexual contact and immediate danger. What is the best general response? \
A. Blame the learner \
B. Promise secrecy regardless of safety \
C. Ignore the danger \
D. Listen, seek immediate local safety and involve appropriate emergency or safeguarding support],
  [Unwanted contact is not the learner's fault. Immediate safety and appropriate safeguarding support are priorities. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Appearance is not a test")[
  STIs may have no obvious symptoms; a person's appearance or reputation does not establish infection status. Consent also cannot be inferred from appearance or silence.
]

